  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-argument essay (with a Terminator-movie framing device running throughout) aimed at engineering and security teams skeptical that \"agentic AI threats\" are hype. Core thesis: no single AI breakthrough is coming; instead two ordinary compounding curves multiply — agent *endurance* (METR's task-length doubling every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one orchestrator coordinating dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code ran an espionage campaign across ~30 targets). The threat emerges in the product of the two curves, which scales faster than linear human security review. A key economic argument (\"LLMjacking,\" per Sysdig): attackers using stolen cloud credentials make capability free for themselves while victims pay the compute bill, breaking the industry's assumption that the capability user is the payer. Finally, swarm economics defeat per-attempt failure-rate reasoning: cheap automated attempts (1−(1−p)^n math) mean the attacker only needs to be right once. Recurring metaphors: the Terminator's \"absolutely will not stop\" reinterpreted as \"will not stop billing,\" Skynet-as-distributed-system, and \"the robot is an API call.\"",
